WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 9 made culture a component of the COSO enterprise framework in its own right. Chapter 14 showed rationalisation as the side of the fraud triangle that culture attacks. Chapter 15 showed controls hollowing out where nobody believes in them. This chapter is what those three observations have in common.
16.1  What corporate culture is
Corporate culture is the set of shared assumptions, values and norms that determine how people in an organisation behave — particularly when no rule covers the situation and nobody is watching.
Four features of the definition are examinable.
It is about what is shared. An individual's conscience is not culture; culture is what people believe about each other's behaviour and about what the organisation expects. Its strongest expression is the phrase "that is how things are done here", which no document produces.
It operates where rules do not. A rule tells you what to do in a situation somebody anticipated. Culture decides everything else, which is most of what happens.
It is learned, chiefly by observation. New employees learn the culture from what they see rewarded, tolerated and punished in their first weeks, not from the induction pack. A single visible inconsistency teaches more than a year of training.
And it exists whether or not anybody designed it. Every organisation has a culture; the only question is whether it is the one the board thinks it has.
Three levels are worth distinguishing, because an exam question that asks why a company with good values behaves badly is asking about the gap between them.
Artefacts: what can be seen — the office, the hierarchy, the language, the documents, the code on the wall.
Espoused values: what the company says it believes, in its statements and its code.
Underlying assumptions: what people actually believe about how the organisation works — whether bad news is welcome, whether the rules apply to everybody, whether a refusal will be supported. This level determines behaviour and it is invisible in documentation.
How culture shapes ethical decisions, which is the first outcome's second half, runs through four mechanisms.
It defines what is normal, and most people do what is normal. The strongest predictor of whether somebody will do something questionable is whether they believe others around them do it.
It determines what can be said. A culture in which raising a problem is treated as disloyalty produces silence, and silence is where every large failure begins.
It sets the cost of refusing. Chapter 15 observed that a professional who must refuse on personal judgment alone is in a weak position; culture decides whether they are supported.
And it supplies or removes rationalisations. "Everybody does it" is a statement about culture, and it is either true or obviously false depending on the organisation.
