16.3  Core values
Core values are the small number of commitments an organisation treats as non-negotiable — the ones that do not change with the strategy, the market or the year, and that the code and the culture are both supposed to express.
Four tests distinguish a core value from a slogan, and an exam answer that gives them is answering the outcome.
Would we hold it even if it cost us money? A value that is abandoned the first time it is expensive was never core, and the only evidence that a value is real is a decision that was made differently because of it.
Is it specific to us, or would any company say it? "Integrity" appears in almost every corporate values statement, including those of companies that have collapsed in fraud. A value that nobody would deny is not distinguishing anything.
Does it decide anything? A usable value resolves a real trade-off: between speed and safety, between a sale and a disclosure, between a cost and a supplier's working conditions.
And is it few enough to be remembered? Three to five values are usable; twelve are a list nobody can recall, which means they cannot be applied at the moment of decision.
How core values promote ethical behaviour, which is the outcome's second half, works through four channels.
They give a reason, not just a rule. A code says what to do; a value says why, which is what allows somebody to extend it to a situation the code did not anticipate.
They are a decision filter under pressure. At the point where Chapter 14's dilemma arrives, a value that has been stated, repeated and visibly applied is available as a reference; one that has not is not.
They are the basis of consistency, across countries as Chapter 15 described and across levels as Chapter 17 insists.
And they attract and retain the people who share them, which over time changes who the organisation consists of — the slowest and most powerful mechanism of all.
Orontes's are an illustration worth having in mind: we do not sell what we would not eat; we tell the customer before they find out; we pay suppliers on the day we agreed; and nobody is asked to do something they would have to hide. Each of those decides something, each is capable of costing money, and none of them is "integrity".
