16.4  What ethical values are worth
The fourth outcome asks for the ways ethical values benefit an organisation, and a good answer runs from the measurable to the strategic rather than beginning with reputation.
Lower losses from misconduct. Chapter 12's fictitious supplier took 261,000, which at Orontes's net margin requires 261,000 ÷ 0.0707 = 3,691,655 of sales to replace. Fraud, safety failures, regulatory penalties and litigation all fall where conduct standards are real.
Lower cost of control. This is the benefit boards find most surprising. A company whose people will not do certain things needs fewer controls to prevent them doing those things, and controls are expensive in money and in friction — approvals, delays, duplicated effort. Ethics and control are substitutes at the margin as well as complements, as Chapter 15 showed.
Better retention and recruitment. People leave organisations that ask them to do things they dislike, and the good ones leave first because they can. If Orontes's turnover across 400 employees falls from 18 to 12 per cent, that is 400 × 0.06 = 24 fewer departures, and at a replacement cost of 9,000 each in recruitment, training and lost productivity, 24 × 9,000 = 216,000 a year.
Higher engagement and better information. In an organisation where bad news is safe to deliver, management finds out about problems early, which is worth more than any control: Chapter 8's event identification depends entirely on people being willing to say what they know.
Customer and supplier trust, which shows up as retention, as willingness to deal on better terms, and as access to customers whose own procurement standards require it.
Lower cost of capital and better access to it. Chapter 4 put twenty basis points on Orontes's 26,892,975 of capital at 26,892,975 × 0.002 = 53,786 a year, and lenders, insurers and investors all price governance and conduct.
Regulatory treatment. Regulators and prosecutors in most jurisdictions take compliance programmes, self-reporting and cooperation into account, and the difference between a penalty for a company with a credible programme and one without is frequently larger than the cost of the programme.
And the avoidance of catastrophic reputational loss, which is last in the list because it is the least measurable and not the least important: reputation takes years to build, is lost in days, and is the asset that does not appear on the balance sheet Book 1 analysed.
Two honest qualifications belong with the list, and strong answers include them.
Most of these benefits are probabilistic and long-dated, while the costs of ethical behaviour are immediate and certain — the 230,135 delay of Chapter 15, the order not taken, the customer not kept. That asymmetry is exactly Chapter 4's measurement problem, and it is why these decisions must be made on values rather than on a business case.
And the causation is not one-way: profitable companies can afford better conduct, so some of the observed relationship between ethics and performance runs backwards. The honest claim is not that ethics pays, but that it costs less than most boards think and that the failures cost very much more.
