LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Values", "culture" and "tone"
Three words that are used loosely and mean different things. Values are what an organisation commits to; culture is what it does; tone, which Chapter 17 takes up, is what its leaders signal. A company can have good values, a poor culture and an inconsistent tone all at once, and an answer that uses the three as synonyms cannot describe the problem. English has a further trap in the word value itself, which in finance means worth and in ethics means commitment — and a sentence such as "our values create value" is doing something slightly dishonest, because it suggests the commitment is held for the worth it produces, which is the opposite of what makes it a commitment. Arabic القيم and French valeurs carry the same double meaning and the same risk. The cleanest test in any language is the one from section 16.3: name the decision that went the other way because of the value, and if there is none, the word is doing no work.
