Chapter summary
Figure F816-03 brings the chapter together.
Figure F816-03. Chapter 16 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Corporate culture is the shared assumptions, values and norms that decide behaviour where no rule applies and nobody is watching; it is learned by observation, it exists whether or not anybody designed it, and it operates at three levels — artefacts, espoused values and the underlying assumptions that actually govern conduct — shaping ethical decisions by defining what is normal, determining what can be said, setting the cost of refusing and supplying or removing rationalisations. A code of conduct makes the standard explicit, settles hard cases before the pressure arrives, supports the individual who must refuse, tells outsiders what to expect, grounds consistent enforcement and is increasingly expected by law — provided it is specific rather than sloganeering, covers the situations people meet, says whom to tell, applies to everybody with the senior named first, is readable and translated, is trained through situations, and is enforced; a code that is visibly ignored is worse than none. Core values are the few non-negotiable commitments that survive costing money, that distinguish this company, that decide real trade-offs and that are few enough to recall — giving the reason rather than the rule, serving as a filter under pressure, grounding consistency and changing over time who the organisation consists of. Their benefits run from lower losses — 261,000 needing 261,000 ÷ 0.0707 = 3,691,655 of sales to replace — through a lower cost of control, better retention worth 24 × 9,000 = 216,000 on 400 × 0.06 = 24 fewer departures, better information, customer and supplier trust, 26,892,975 × 0.002 = 53,786 of capital cost, regulatory treatment and the avoidance of reputational collapse, qualified honestly by the fact that the costs are immediate and the benefits probabilistic. And all of it fails in one way: a gap between stated and lived values, visible in unread codes, inconsistent discipline, slow bad news, departing complainants and compliance measured by completion rates.
