Case-style set: the values exercise
What the evidence indicates. No single item here is a finding; the pattern is. One concern in two years from four hundred people is not an absence of problems but an absence of reporting, and the subject of the one concern — car parking — tells you what people believe is safe to raise. The two disciplinary outcomes are the most damaging item: a senior manager's expense irregularities resolved quietly and a warehouse employee dismissed for 400 of stock teaches everybody which rules apply to whom, and that lesson destroys exactly the consistency that Chapter 14 identified as the enemy of rationalisation. A two-month gap in a reconciliation that nobody reported shows that reporting a control deficiency is not expected behaviour, although Chapter 13 makes it an obligation. And eleven quarters within 1 per cent of forecast is improbable enough to require an explanation: either the business is extraordinarily predictable or results are being managed, and Book 1's earnings quality analysis would be the place to find out which.
What I would do about the code and the values. Reduce both. Six values nobody can recall and twenty-four pages nobody has read are not instruments; they are artefacts. I would cut the values to three or four that decide actual trade-offs, each stated as a commitment that could cost money, and cut the code to the situations people meet, with specific thresholds and a named route for concerns. Then I would do the thing that matters more than either: decide a hard case visibly and explain it, because a culture is made by the decisions where a value cost something.
What I would measure instead of acknowledgement. Six things: the number of concerns raised and what happened to each; the proportion of employees who say they could raise one without consequence, asked anonymously; how long it took for the last three pieces of bad news to reach management, and whether they came from inside; whether discipline was consistent across levels, reviewed case by case; whether the inventory reconciliation and the other key controls were performed on time; and how many decisions in the year were taken that cost money for a stated reason, with those reasons published internally. Acknowledgement at 100 per cent measures that a form was signed.
